//! S12 — a Sigil keyword IS legitimately an identifier, in field position.
//!
//! `sigil migrate`'s keyword-identifier pass renames every keyword found in a
//! binding position, on the stated premise that "a keyword can never legitimately
//! be an identifier". The premise is false: the parser accepts most keywords as
//! struct field names, in both the declaration and the literal.
//!
//! These five parse today and need no rename.

Σ Accepted {
    anima: Int,
    state: Int,
    body: Int,
    layer: Int,
    scope: Int,
}

rite main() {
    ≔ a = Accepted { anima: 1, state: 2, body: 3, layer: 4, scope: 5 };
}

// These two do NOT parse as field names, and are the cases the pass exists for:
//
//     Σ Rejected { aspect: Int }   // expected identifier, found Trait
//     Σ Rejected { alter: Int }    // expected identifier, found Alter
//
// So the rename is right for `aspect` and `alter` and wrong for the five above.
// Expected once fixed: the pass consults which keywords the parser actually
// rejects in the position it found the name, instead of renaming all of them.
